text:
Adversarial training (AT) is a powerful regularization technique for neural networks that enhances their robustness to input perturbations. However, its specific effects on natural language processing are still not well understood. In this paper, we present and analyze a neural POS tagging model that leverages AT. Our experiments on the Penn Treebank WSJ corpus and the Universal Dependencies (UD) dataset (27 languages) show that AT not only increases overall tagging accuracy, but also:

1) effectively prevents overfitting in low-resource languages;
2) enhances tagging accuracy for rare and unseen words;
3) contributes to the improvement of dependency parsing performance in downstream tasks; and
4) leads to cleaner word representations learned by the model.

Our results demonstrate that AT is a generally effective approach for different sequence labeling tasks and motivate further exploration of its use in natural language processing.
